BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 237.78 Tm (Cash timing, not demand, is the trap that kills most first-year operators. Nemiton warns that Medicaid pays in) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 222.55 Tm (30 to 60 days, that billing can drag 45 to 90 days after the trip, and that manual billing becomes) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 207.33 Tm (unmanageable past 20 trips a month. The floor, per Nemiton, is 90 days of operating cash on hand) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 192.10 Tm ([w246jND2Ahw @ 00:06:12]. NEMT Launch Blueprint gives the calendar version: trips run in January may) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 176.88 Tm (not pay until March, and that gap is what kills new businesses, so budget for the reserve before launch) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 161.65 Tm ([4_YiCCE47fY @ 00:04:54]. Joe Padilla lived the worst case. A $15K receivable went unpaid. Nursing) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 146.43 Tm (homes pay in about 30 days while contracts take 40 to 60. A client running 3,000 to 4,000 trips a day had) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 131.20 Tm (roughly $1M frozen when a broker went bankrupt, and Padilla sold a truck for $15K just to make payroll.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 115.98 Tm (Padilla's fix is standing lines of credit [g6k_yncRR8o @ 00:23:38]. Shantasia_RN names the mental error) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 100.75 Tm (behind all of this: treating the bank balance as available money. Money in the business account is not) Tj ET
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 54.00 85.53 Tm (automatically owner income. Reconcile billed trips against paid trips every cycle, and before any big move) Tj ET
